  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Sunday: Due to a low pressure system retrograding off the \nPNW coast, the Inland Northwest will remain in southwesterly flow \nfor the next couple days. This will provide PWATs 100 to 150 percent \nof normal (0.75 to 1 inch). Small disturbances passing through will \nprovide enough instability combined with daytime heating alongside \nadded moisture to result in chances for thunderstorms each afternoon \nand evening this weekend. For this afternoon and evening, much of \nthe northern mountains and valleys, Cascades, Palouse, Spokane/CdA, \nand ID Panhandle have a 10 to 20 percent chance of thunderstorms. \nToday's soundings indicate elevated PWATs 0.60 inches and above and \nDCAPE values near 1000 J/kg, favoring gusty outflow winds near 40 \nmph. Additionally, lightning induced fire starts are possible. These \nhigher PWATs will keep fire concerns at elevated rather than \ncritical for all areas but the Lower Palouse and Snake River. For \nthis zone (709), a Red Flag Warning has been issued through 9pm. \nMain impacts in this area include lightning occurring in an area \nthat has been dry, especially away from any heavy rain cores, and \ngusty outflow winds that could increase fire spread. Tomorrow, \nsoundings show thunderstorms with similar characteristics and \nthreats as today, but highest chances will be further confined to \nthe mountains. Areas above that had a 10 to 20 percent chance of \nthunderstorms will see chances much closer to 10 percent. \n\nMonday through Friday: The low off the PNW coast will remain there \nthrough the middle of the week, resulting in higher heights building \nover the region to start the work week. With waves still moving \nthrough, chances of precipitation and isolated thunderstorms remain \nMonday and Tuesday, but are confined only to the mountains with \nareas elsewhere having near zero chances. These chances will taper \noff after Tuesday as a warmer and drier air mass moves in. Near the \nend of the week, a quarter of the clusters show the low moving \nnortheastwards and towards the region, which would favor a cooler, \nwetter end of the week. However, 75 percent of the clusters favor \ncontinued ridging over the area as the offshore low weakens. \nTemperatures will remain at or just above normal through mid-week, \nthen warm to 4-6 degrees warmer than normal as ridging remains over \nthe area. /AS",
